people include zucchini  delicata acorn jack-o-lantern pumpkins  there's a lot of diversity there  the crop that  is the third major crop in the u.s  there's other crops eaten globally is  cucurbita moshada this is a rather  tropical squash  they tend to have day link sensitivities  they uh have short day squash  they tend to be grown and found in  equatorial regions they need much more  heat units to really ripen and mature in  general  but the other great thing for me as i've  started to work on them is for someone  like me in the middle of new york state  if i start working with these now and in  collaboration with some rei organic  research extension initiative projects  we've had  we can link  uh what our  breeding work is in new york to  what the problems are on the ground for  sustainable ecological growers tennessee  and south all the way down to the  southern coast  so that we can see what are people doing  to combat  climate change related changes uh now  what are the challenges they have on  their farm and incorporate it into  our cultivars today is i'm always trying  to predict the future we're always  looking ahead a few years and for me as  we look at as our growing zones  become warmer growing zones what that  means to me is the pests and pathogens  of more southerly zones will be moving  north and  a way to get ahead of that is i'm  looking ahead in my crystal ball to what  should i have developed 10 years from  now  is going to be looking to see what  people are  dealing with in a climate south of us  so the big challenges they face  uh among many but the ones that kind of  are relevant to what i'll share  is  it's  we can pick on our winters our gray days  in ithaca uh it doesn't make for a great  light for a photo shoot though doesn't  it and  right but we have our freezing winter is  an agricultural reset so all the pests  and pathogens get wiped out the season  ends we stop growing the host plants and  we go into the spring we're really  starting fresh with a blank slate with  all the accumulated  biotic stresses  wiped clean  south of us  in the southeastern u.s they don't have  that luxury of winter really as much to  kind of wipe the slate clean  so when they try to grow some other  squash like cucurbita pepo or maxima  you know we grow so much zucchini it's  really challenging to grow there the  cucurbita pepo because it's vulnerable  to all the threats  cucurbita moshada is  much less vulnerable because it's coming  from tropical regions of the world where  it has to deal with these pests and  pathogens year round  and so as cucurbita dispersed from its  centers of origins in the americas to  being  having all these global iterations and  his cultures reinterpreting it to their  cuisine their culture their growing  region we have all these great secondary  centers where people have reselected the  cucurbits we know so in the u.s when you  think of kikura moshada uh the the one  you're probably familiar with is  butternut squash  it has a relative on the caribbean  calabazza squash tropical pumpkin  is another name for it so that's  commonly grown there  when  these squashes and arrived in europe  they made it down to italy and were  reselected for other traits so our  butternut squash in  italy one of the variations became this  trombonchino and so a tromboncino squash  can also work as a winter squash a  mature squash like a butternut it also  works immature kind of like a zucchini  stage but it's much tastier because the  seed we have all the moisture is in this  seed ball at the end like what butternut  squash would be  it has this exaggerated neck that's  actually very  dense tissue that has a much better  flavor than a zucchini it has much more  food than a zucchini it's less watery  and  it tastes great and different than  butternut squash and that when we would  take this we'll slice it a light brine  and then sear it it's really delicious  and i didn't know that until i started  working with s